From 07559b65bb3fa61ee505692648a4077a1947ecde Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:09:33 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Keep replica projections per origin scope and share
 slim decoded stages
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Problem: mini idle Desktop serve used 0.6–1.2 cores. Alternating five
profile scopes staged all 18,264 replica task rows on each read.
Why 1: annotation holder and profile=all polls alternate origins; prior
fixture measured 976 ms CPU/read vs 31 ms for one repeated origin.
Why 2: projection _states had one slot per path; project discarded it
when the gateway/profile changed. Remote read memo was single-slot too.
Root (happened): cache identity did not match the multi-profile consumer.
Root (not caught): incremental tests exercised one origin; the initial
serve fixture omitted profile configs and never reproduced scope turnover.
Recurrence: incremental/slim-row changes 60800c97577 and 14aa48d2c4c
preserved single-path state. This fixes multi-profile reuse, not refresh
cadence or semantic fingerprint narrowing (owned by annotation card B).

Retain bounded (replica path, gateway, origin profile) states and memos.
Share clock-free slim decoded rows only across origins they do not link;
linked origins retain whole records. Reconcile deleted shared rows against
the replica even when old scopes have been evicted.

Proof: 33 tests passed in test_kanban_replica_profile_states.py (6),
test_kanban_replica_incremental.py (10), test_kanban_replica_read_memo.py
(12), test_kanban_replica_rows_memory.py (5), all under tests/hermes_cli.
The new turnover/deletion test failed before its correction.
Full isolated serve replay on identical mini board backups, same core
baseline ac1110c097, same polls and seeded writes: 41.50 CPU s/90 wall s
before vs 6.78/90 after. No live backend signalled or restarted.
Replay omits external mod routes; measured reduction is fixture-only.
Card t_01a1d6d42877f719 retains harness/raw outputs and owns live readback.
